onale": "An open Republican-leaning seat collides with a strongly Democratic national environment, leaving the race close to even. Raters split evenly across Lean R, Tossup, and Lean D, the lone recent poll shows a one-point contest, and markets tilt narrowly Democratic, so the forecast holds a slight Democratic edge with wide uncertainty.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling, movement in the national environment, or the lobbying-story attacks on Mitchell gaining traction could tip the race either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 69 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Ashley Hinson (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Iowa's 2nd District is an open seat after Republican Ashley Hinson left to run for Senate, and the contest between Republican Joe Mitchell and Democrat Lindsay James sits close to even. The district leans Republican by about four points, but the national midterm environment favors Democrats, and the two forces largely cancel out. Nine expert ratings split evenly: three lean Republican, three call it a toss-up, and three lean toward a Democratic flip.\n\nPublic polling is sparse — the newest survey, a Democratic-sponsored July poll, showed a one-point race — so uncertainty is high. Fundraising is nearly matched, with Mitchell slightly ahead in cash while Democratic-leaning outside groups spend against him. Prediction markets give Democrats a modest edge. The forecast lands at a narrow Democratic margin with wide uncertainty, a genuine toss-up that could break either way.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5595, "margin": 0.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5585, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6246, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.619, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5799, "margin": 1.8}]}